1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,075
Every structure that vibrates, rotates,

2
00:00:03,125 --> 00:00:06,637
heats up or cools down is quietly counting
load cycles.

3
00:00:07,137 --> 00:00:11,400
Fatigue, failure under repeated loads well
below the static strength,

4
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:16,525
is behind a large share of the mechanical
and structural failures seen in service.

5
00:00:17,024 --> 00:00:22,107
And fatigue has two very different faces. In
high-cycle fatigue the material

6
00:00:22,157 --> 00:00:27,308
stays elastic and survives millions of
cycles. In low-cycle fatigue it yields

7
00:00:27,358 --> 00:00:31,495
on every cycle, and may fail after only
hundreds or thousands.

8
00:00:31,995 --> 00:00:34,872
In this video we look at what separates the
two,

9
00:00:34,922 --> 00:00:38,286
the methods used to predict fatigue life in
each regime,

10
00:00:38,336 --> 00:00:42,249
and a question that confuses many analysts:
what does accumulated

11
00:00:42,299 --> 00:00:47,310
plastic strain in a finite element model
really tell you, and when is it a problem?

12
00:00:47,810 --> 00:00:51,630
Everything is backed by nonlinear finite
element simulations,

13
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:57,910
run with a cyclic plasticity solver written
for this study and checked against an
independent code.

14
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:03,839
Fatigue damage starts at the scale of the
grains. Under cyclic shear,

15
00:01:03,889 --> 00:01:09,039
dislocations organise into persistent slip
bands. Where these bands meet the free

16
00:01:09,089 --> 00:01:14,238
surface they push out tiny extrusions and
intrusions, which act as micro-notches.

17
00:01:14,738 --> 00:01:18,488
A crack then grows a few grains deep along
the slip planes.

18
00:01:18,538 --> 00:01:23,060
That is stage one. It then turns
perpendicular to the largest principal

19
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:26,925
stress and advances a little every cycle.
That is stage two,

20
00:01:26,975 --> 00:01:32,399
and it leaves striations and, on a larger
scale, beach marks on the fracture surface.

21
00:01:32,899 --> 00:01:35,894
Finally the remaining ligament can no longer
carry the

22
00:01:35,944 --> 00:01:38,770
peak load, and it breaks in a single fast
fracture.

23
00:01:39,270 --> 00:01:42,779
So fatigue life is crack initiation plus
crack growth.

24
00:01:42,829 --> 00:01:47,984
Smooth parts in high-cycle fatigue spend
most of their life initiating a crack.

25
00:01:48,034 --> 00:01:54,113
At sharp notches, at welds, and in low-cycle
fatigue, crack growth takes a much larger
share.

26
00:01:54,613 --> 00:01:58,758
To describe a load cycle we use the maximum
and minimum stress,

27
00:01:58,808 --> 00:02:03,885
the stress range delta sigma, the amplitude
sigma a, which is half the range,

28
00:02:03,935 --> 00:02:09,079
the mean stress sigma m, and the stress
ratio R, the minimum over the maximum.

29
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:14,473
R equals minus one is fully reversed
loading. R equals zero is pulsating tension.

30
00:02:16,023 --> 00:02:19,726
So what really separates low-cycle from
high-cycle fatigue?

31
00:02:19,776 --> 00:02:23,097
It is not a magic number of cycles. It is
plasticity.

32
00:02:23,597 --> 00:02:29,387
Plot stress against strain over one cycle.
At a small amplitude the response is a
straight

33
00:02:29,437 --> 00:02:34,708
line: elastic, with no energy dissipated.
Push the amplitude past the cyclic yield

34
00:02:34,758 --> 00:02:40,353
point and the line opens into a hysteresis
loop. Its width is the plastic strain range,

35
00:02:40,403 --> 00:02:44,506
and its area is the plastic work dissipated,
every single cycle.

36
00:02:45,006 --> 00:02:49,099
When the plastic strain is large compared
with the elastic strain,

37
00:02:49,149 --> 00:02:54,057
we are in low-cycle fatigue, with lives up
to roughly ten thousand to a hundred

38
00:02:54,107 --> 00:02:58,137
thousand cycles, and strain is the variable
that controls damage.

39
00:02:58,187 --> 00:03:04,414
When plastic strain is negligible, we are in
high-cycle fatigue, and stress is the
natural variable.

40
00:03:04,914 --> 00:03:09,404
You can see this on the strain–life curve.
On log–log axes the elastic

41
00:03:09,454 --> 00:03:12,842
strain amplitude falls along a shallow
straight line,

42
00:03:12,892 --> 00:03:17,123
Basquin's law, and the plastic strain
amplitude along a steep one,

43
00:03:17,173 --> 00:03:21,274
the Coffin–Manson law. Their sum is the
total strain–life curve.

44
00:03:21,774 --> 00:03:24,911
Where the two lines cross is the transition
life.

45
00:03:24,961 --> 00:03:29,334
To its left, plasticity dominates and life
is governed by ductility.

46
00:03:29,384 --> 00:03:34,017
To its right, elastic strain dominates and
life is governed by strength.

47
00:03:34,067 --> 00:03:38,505
For the S355-type steel used in this video
the transition is at about

48
00:03:38,555 --> 00:03:43,709
three thousand three hundred cycles. High-
strength steels reach it much earlier.

49
00:03:44,209 --> 00:03:48,312
Beyond about ten million cycles lies the
very-high-cycle regime,

50
00:03:48,362 --> 00:03:52,011
where cracks often start below the surface
at inclusions,

51
00:03:52,061 --> 00:03:59,084
leaving a characteristic fish-eye. That is
why many modern standards no longer assume a
true endurance limit.

52
00:03:59,584 --> 00:04:04,416
And remember: in a real component, the bulk
can stay elastic while a notch

53
00:04:04,466 --> 00:04:09,232
root yields. The surrounding elastic
material then imposes a strain cycle

54
00:04:09,282 --> 00:04:13,190
on the notch. That is why local strain
methods work so well,

55
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:17,610
and why finite element analysis sits at the
centre of both regimes.

56
00:04:19,160 --> 00:04:22,437
High-cycle fatigue first: the world of
stress and life.

57
00:04:22,937 --> 00:04:26,952
Since Wöhler's railway axle experiments in
the eighteen-sixties,

58
00:04:27,002 --> 00:04:31,588
the basic tool has been the S–N curve:
stress amplitude against cycles to

59
00:04:31,638 --> 00:04:35,081
failure on logarithmic axes. In the finite-
life region,

60
00:04:35,131 --> 00:04:39,654
Basquin's equation makes it a straight line:
the stress amplitude equals

61
00:04:39,704 --> 00:04:45,179
the fatigue strength coefficient times two N
to the power b, a small negative exponent.

62
00:04:45,679 --> 00:04:50,232
Many steels show a knee and a fatigue limit,
for smooth polished specimens

63
00:04:50,282 --> 00:04:54,649
typically around half the tensile strength,
reached between one and ten

64
00:04:54,699 --> 00:04:58,381
million cycles. Aluminium alloys show no
clear limit at all.

65
00:04:58,431 --> 00:05:03,047
Test data from small polished specimens is
then reduced for surface finish,

66
00:05:03,097 --> 00:05:06,655
size, load type, temperature and the
reliability you need.

67
00:05:07,155 --> 00:05:10,704
Next, mean stress. Tension opens cracks and
shortens life,

68
00:05:10,754 --> 00:05:15,854
compression helps. The Haigh diagram plots
the allowable amplitude against the mean

69
00:05:15,904 --> 00:05:20,322
stress. Goodman's line joins the fatigue
limit to the ultimate strength,

70
00:05:20,372 --> 00:05:25,410
Gerber uses a parabola, and Soderberg's line
ends at the yield strength and is the

71
00:05:25,460 --> 00:05:32,050
most conservative. Smith, Watson and Topper
combine maximum stress and amplitude into
one damage parameter.

72
00:05:32,550 --> 00:05:36,284
Then notches. The elastic stress
concentration factor, K t,

73
00:05:36,334 --> 00:05:41,671
multiplies the nominal stress at the notch
root. Our plate with a central hole gives

74
00:05:41,721 --> 00:05:46,866
K t equal to three point two five on the
gross section, or two point four four on

75
00:05:46,916 --> 00:05:52,574
the net section, matching the classical
Peterson value to within a fraction of a
percent.

76
00:05:53,074 --> 00:05:56,846
The fatigue strength is reduced by less than
K t suggests,

77
00:05:56,896 --> 00:06:01,524
because a steep stress gradient affects only
a tiny volume of material.

78
00:06:01,574 --> 00:06:06,598
The fatigue notch factor, K f, and
Peterson's notch sensitivity capture this.

79
00:06:06,648 --> 00:06:13,385
For a twenty millimetre hole in steel the
notch sensitivity is close to one, so K f is
practically K t.

80
00:06:13,885 --> 00:06:18,350
Welded joints need their own toolbox. The
weld toe is a sharp notch

81
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:21,449
with tensile residual stresses close to
yield,

82
00:06:21,499 --> 00:06:26,368
which is also why weld S–N curves largely
ignore the applied mean stress.

83
00:06:26,868 --> 00:06:31,534
With the nominal stress method you find your
detail in a catalogue of detail

84
00:06:31,584 --> 00:06:35,505
categories, such as FAT 80, and use a simple
beam-theory stress.

85
00:06:35,555 --> 00:06:39,601
It only works if your joint and loading
match a catalogued detail.

86
00:06:40,100 --> 00:06:44,054
The structural hot-spot method uses a finite
element model.

87
00:06:44,104 --> 00:06:49,618
Here is a fillet-welded attachment in
bending. The surface stress rises toward the

88
00:06:49,668 --> 00:06:54,368
weld toe: a linear structural part plus a
sharp, nonlinear notch peak.

89
00:06:54,418 --> 00:07:00,204
We read the surface stress at half a plate
thickness and at one and a half thicknesses

90
00:07:00,254 --> 00:07:05,430
from the toe, and extrapolate linearly to
the toe, as DNV-RP-C203 recommends.

91
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:13,030
The result, ninety-nine megapascals, is the
hot-spot stress, used with a hot-spot S–N
curve such as DNV curve D.

92
00:07:13,531 --> 00:07:17,087
The effective notch stress method goes one
step further.

93
00:07:17,137 --> 00:07:20,886
The toe is rounded with a fictitious one
millimetre radius,

94
00:07:20,936 --> 00:07:28,612
the peak stress is computed directly, here
two hundred and seventy-one megapascals, and
compared with the FAT 225 curve.

95
00:07:29,112 --> 00:07:34,046
For this joint the routes agree: about one
point one to one point five million

96
00:07:34,096 --> 00:07:37,433
cycles for a hundred megapascal nominal
stress range.

97
00:07:37,483 --> 00:07:43,440
But each method has its own rules for
meshing and read-out, and they must be
followed exactly.

98
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:48,807
Real loads are rarely constant. Rainflow
counting breaks an irregular history

99
00:07:48,857 --> 00:07:52,191
into closed cycles, each with its own range
and mean.

100
00:07:52,241 --> 00:07:56,341
Each cycle uses up a fraction n over N of
the life at that range,

101
00:07:56,391 --> 00:08:01,002
and the Palmgren–Miner rule adds the
fractions. Failure is predicted when

102
00:08:01,052 --> 00:08:05,919
the sum reaches one, and design codes add
margin with design fatigue factors.

103
00:08:06,419 --> 00:08:11,447
Miner's rule ignores load-sequence effects,
and multiaxial stresses need

104
00:08:11,497 --> 00:08:15,185
more: invariant criteria such as Sines and
Crossland,

105
00:08:15,235 --> 00:08:20,686
the mesoscopic Dang Van criterion, or
critical-plane criteria such as Findley.

106
00:08:22,236 --> 00:08:25,044
Now low-cycle fatigue, where strain takes
over.

107
00:08:25,544 --> 00:08:30,307
Under strain-controlled cycling, metals
evolve. Soft, annealed materials harden

108
00:08:30,357 --> 00:08:34,754
cyclically, while cold-worked and high-
strength materials tend to soften.

109
00:08:34,804 --> 00:08:39,506
After a while the loops stabilise, and the
tips of the stable loops define the

110
00:08:39,556 --> 00:08:44,380
cyclic stress–strain curve, usually written
in Ramberg–Osgood form with a cyclic

111
00:08:44,430 --> 00:08:48,340
strength coefficient, K prime, and a
hardening exponent, n prime.

112
00:08:48,840 --> 00:08:53,715
Many steels show Masing behaviour: each
hysteresis branch is the cyclic curve

113
00:08:53,765 --> 00:08:58,001
scaled by a factor of two, so any loop can
be built from one curve.

114
00:08:58,501 --> 00:09:01,860
Life follows the Coffin–Manson–Basquin
relation.

115
00:09:01,910 --> 00:09:05,198
The total strain amplitude is the elastic
part,

116
00:09:05,248 --> 00:09:12,513
sigma f prime over E times two N to the b,
plus the plastic part, epsilon f prime times
two N to the c.

117
00:09:13,013 --> 00:09:17,277
Mean stress enters through Morrow's
correction, which subtracts the mean

118
00:09:17,327 --> 00:09:22,070
stress from the fatigue strength
coefficient, or through the
Smith–Watson–Topper

119
00:09:22,120 --> 00:09:25,485
parameter: the maximum stress times the
strain amplitude.

120
00:09:25,985 --> 00:09:30,821
How do we find the strain at a notch root?
Neuber's rule says the local stress

121
00:09:30,871 --> 00:09:35,708
times the local strain equals the elastic
value, K t times the nominal stress,

122
00:09:35,758 --> 00:09:40,594
squared, divided by E. That is a hyperbola,
which we intersect with the cyclic

123
00:09:40,644 --> 00:09:44,415
curve. Glinka's rule equates strain energy
densities instead.

124
00:09:44,465 --> 00:09:48,675
Neuber tends to overestimate the strain,
Glinka to underestimate it.

125
00:09:49,175 --> 00:09:54,145
Or we run an elastic-plastic finite element
analysis with a cyclic material model.

126
00:09:54,195 --> 00:09:58,614
In our notched plate at plus or minus one
hundred and eighty megapascals,

127
00:09:58,664 --> 00:10:03,267
the computed strain amplitude at the hole is
zero point three eight percent,

128
00:10:03,317 --> 00:10:08,288
neatly between Glinka at zero point three
two and Neuber at zero point four three.

129
00:10:08,338 --> 00:10:11,961
The strain–life curve then gives about four
thousand cycles.

130
00:10:12,461 --> 00:10:15,898
Other approaches use the hysteresis energy
per cycle,

131
00:10:15,948 --> 00:10:19,845
or critical-plane strain parameters such as
Brown–Miller and

132
00:10:19,895 --> 00:10:23,595
Fatemi–Socie for multiaxial and non-
proportional loading.

133
00:10:24,096 --> 00:10:28,171
Design codes package this. ASME Section
eight, Division two,

134
00:10:28,221 --> 00:10:34,153
offers an elastic route, where the stress
range is amplified by a plasticity
correction

135
00:10:34,203 --> 00:10:39,585
factor, K e, once it exceeds the shakedown
limit, and an elastic-plastic route,

136
00:10:39,635 --> 00:10:45,360
where an effective strain range, the elastic
stress range over E plus the equivalent

137
00:10:45,410 --> 00:10:50,173
plastic strain range from a cyclic analysis,
enters the fatigue curve.

138
00:10:50,223 --> 00:10:55,880
DNV-RP-C203 likewise corrects elastic hot-
spot stresses for plasticity in low-cycle

139
00:10:55,930 --> 00:11:00,280
fatigue, and adds the low-cycle damage to
the high-cycle damage.

140
00:11:01,830 --> 00:11:06,401
Let's put this into a finite element model:
a steel plate eighty millimetres

141
00:11:06,451 --> 00:11:10,779
wide with a twenty millimetre hole, modelled
as a quarter with symmetry,

142
00:11:10,829 --> 00:11:15,278
in plane stress, with nine-node quadratic
elements graded toward the hole.

143
00:11:15,778 --> 00:11:20,545
The material is an S355-type steel with a
cyclic proportional limit of two

144
00:11:20,595 --> 00:11:25,947
hundred and fifty megapascals and three
Chaboche nonlinear kinematic back-stresses,

145
00:11:25,997 --> 00:11:29,592
calibrated to the stabilised cyclic
stress–strain curve.

146
00:11:29,642 --> 00:11:33,888
The solver uses implicit return mapping with
a consistent tangent,

147
00:11:33,938 --> 00:11:39,030
and on the same cyclic problem it matches
CalculiX to within about two percent.

148
00:11:39,530 --> 00:11:41,559
Four load cases tell the whole story.

149
00:11:42,059 --> 00:11:47,585
Case one: plus or minus seventy megapascals.
The peak stress at the hole is two hundred

150
00:11:47,635 --> 00:11:51,366
and twenty-seven megapascals, below the
proportional limit,

151
00:11:51,416 --> 00:11:56,814
so everything is elastic. The loop is a
line, the accumulated plastic strain is
zero,

152
00:11:56,864 --> 00:12:02,326
and the strain–life curve gives about five
million cycles. This is high-cycle fatigue.

153
00:12:02,826 --> 00:12:08,194
Case two: zero to one hundred and forty
megapascals. Elastically the hole would see
four

154
00:12:08,244 --> 00:12:12,751
hundred and fifty-five megapascals, so the
first loading yields the notch.

155
00:12:12,801 --> 00:12:17,984
On unloading, the elastic surroundings push
the yielded material into compression and

156
00:12:18,034 --> 00:12:23,033
leave a residual stress. From the second
cycle on, the response is purely elastic.

157
00:12:23,083 --> 00:12:28,328
This is elastic shakedown: the accumulated
plastic strain jumps once, then stays flat.

158
00:12:28,828 --> 00:12:33,436
But look at the local mean stress: plus one
hundred and fifteen megapascals.

159
00:12:33,486 --> 00:12:37,788
With a mean stress correction, the predicted
life drops from five point

160
00:12:37,838 --> 00:12:42,017
six million to between six hundred thousand
and one point six million

161
00:12:42,067 --> 00:12:45,388
cycles. Shakedown removes plastic cycling,
not fatigue.

162
00:12:45,888 --> 00:12:49,576
Case three: plus or minus one hundred and
eighty megapascals.

163
00:12:49,626 --> 00:12:54,233
Now the elastic stress range at the hole,
nearly twelve hundred megapascals,

164
00:12:54,283 --> 00:12:58,952
is far above twice the yield strength, so
yielding reverses every half cycle.

165
00:12:59,002 --> 00:13:02,506
The notch root settles into a closed, fat
hysteresis loop.

166
00:13:02,556 --> 00:13:10,901
This is alternating plasticity, also called
plastic shakedown, and it is the home of
low-cycle fatigue. Life: about four thousand
cycles.

167
00:13:11,401 --> 00:13:15,083
Case four: zero to two hundred and eighty-
five megapascals.

168
00:13:15,133 --> 00:13:19,195
Now the net section yields, and the load
keeps a high mean value.

169
00:13:19,245 --> 00:13:26,596
The loop no longer closes; it walks to the
right, and the plate gets a little longer
every cycle. This is ratcheting.

170
00:13:27,096 --> 00:13:31,139
Now compare the accumulated plastic strain
at the notch root.

171
00:13:31,189 --> 00:13:34,829
Zero for the elastic case. A single step for
shakedown.

172
00:13:34,879 --> 00:13:40,734
A steadily rising line for alternating
plasticity, and a rising line for ratcheting
too.

173
00:13:41,234 --> 00:13:46,052
And that is the catch. Cases three and four
look alike in accumulated plastic

174
00:13:46,102 --> 00:13:51,110
strain, but they are completely different
problems. One is a closed strain cycle

175
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:56,358
that slowly initiates a fatigue crack. The
other is a net strain that keeps growing

176
00:13:56,408 --> 00:14:00,278
until ductility is exhausted or the part
deforms unacceptably.

177
00:14:01,828 --> 00:14:06,567
So what is accumulated plastic strain? In
most finite element codes it is the

178
00:14:06,617 --> 00:14:11,170
equivalent plastic strain, PEEQ in Abaqus:
the time integral of the square

179
00:14:11,220 --> 00:14:15,462
root of two thirds of the plastic strain
rate contracted with itself.

180
00:14:15,962 --> 00:14:20,499
Because it integrates the magnitude of
plastic flow, it can only increase.

181
00:14:20,549 --> 00:14:25,210
Every reversal adds to it, in whatever
direction. Under uniaxial alternating

182
00:14:25,260 --> 00:14:29,302
plasticity it grows by twice the plastic
strain range every cycle.

183
00:14:29,352 --> 00:14:33,331
At our notch root that is zero point eight
two percent per cycle,

184
00:14:33,381 --> 00:14:39,158
while the strain itself just oscillates
between plus and minus zero point three
eight percent.

185
00:14:39,658 --> 00:14:43,500
So a large PEEQ alone does not tell you
whether you have a fatigue

186
00:14:43,550 --> 00:14:47,392
problem, a ratcheting problem, or neither.
You have to look at how

187
00:14:47,442 --> 00:14:50,694
the plastic strain tensor changes over a
complete cycle.

188
00:14:51,194 --> 00:14:55,671
Under repeated loading, a structure settles
into one of four responses.

189
00:14:55,721 --> 00:15:01,028
Elastic, with no yielding at all. Elastic
shakedown, with yielding only in the first

190
00:15:01,078 --> 00:15:05,364
cycles. Alternating plasticity, a closed
plastic cycle that repeats.

191
00:15:05,414 --> 00:15:11,804
And ratcheting, also called incremental
collapse, with a net plastic strain
increment in every cycle.

192
00:15:12,304 --> 00:15:15,823
Melan's theorem gives the condition for
elastic shakedown:

193
00:15:15,873 --> 00:15:20,499
if some constant, self-equilibrating
residual stress field exists that keeps

194
00:15:20,549 --> 00:15:25,052
the elastic stresses plus the residual
stresses within yield at all times,

195
00:15:25,102 --> 00:15:29,605
the structure will shake down. For a
perfectly plastic material that means

196
00:15:29,655 --> 00:15:33,543
the elastic stress range cannot exceed twice
the yield strength,

197
00:15:33,593 --> 00:15:38,465
which is the basis of the ASME limit on the
primary-plus-secondary stress range.

198
00:15:38,964 --> 00:15:42,478
Ratcheting needs two ingredients: a
sustained primary load,

199
00:15:42,528 --> 00:15:46,707
like pressure, and a cyclic secondary
stress, like a thermal gradient.

200
00:15:46,757 --> 00:15:54,016
The classic case is the Bree problem: a
pressurised thin cylinder with a cycling
temperature difference through the wall.

201
00:15:54,516 --> 00:15:58,722
Here Bree's diagram is computed numerically
with a through-wall model:

202
00:15:58,772 --> 00:16:02,674
sixty-five hundred load combinations,
primary stress on one axis,

203
00:16:02,724 --> 00:16:06,626
thermal stress range on the other, each
cycled twenty-four times.

204
00:16:06,676 --> 00:16:11,307
Watch the regimes appear: elastic,
shakedown, alternating plasticity once the

205
00:16:11,357 --> 00:16:15,016
thermal range passes twice yield, and two
ratcheting regions.

206
00:16:15,066 --> 00:16:21,765
The computed map matches Bree's analytical
boundaries at every point but one, which
lies exactly on a boundary.

207
00:16:22,266 --> 00:16:26,765
The same physics appears in our notched
plate. We apply a constant tension,

208
00:16:26,815 --> 00:16:31,557
then cycle a temperature field that is hot
in the centre and cool at the edges.

209
00:16:31,607 --> 00:16:34,954
With a low tension, the hole edge yields
back and forth,

210
00:16:35,004 --> 00:16:39,018
but the length of the plate stops changing:
alternating plasticity.

211
00:16:39,068 --> 00:16:45,145
Double the tension, and the plate grows by
twenty-two micrometres every cycle:
structural ratcheting.

212
00:16:45,645 --> 00:16:48,874
The difference is the elastic core. In the
safe case,

213
00:16:48,924 --> 00:16:53,019
part of the ligament never yields during a
cycle and keeps carrying

214
00:16:53,069 --> 00:16:57,102
the primary load. In the ratcheting case, no
elastic core is left.

215
00:16:57,602 --> 00:17:02,201
Material ratcheting is a separate effect,
and it depends strongly on the hardening

216
00:17:02,251 --> 00:17:06,906
model. Under stress control with a mean
stress, linear kinematic hardening predicts

217
00:17:06,956 --> 00:17:11,385
closed loops and no ratcheting at all. A
single Armstrong–Frederick back-stress

218
00:17:11,435 --> 00:17:15,069
predicts strong ratcheting, almost two
percent in fifteen cycles.

219
00:17:15,119 --> 00:17:19,208
A multi-component Chaboche model lands in
between. If ratcheting matters,

220
00:17:19,258 --> 00:17:23,573
calibrate the model against ratcheting
tests, not just a stress–strain curve.

221
00:17:24,073 --> 00:17:27,972
So how do you assess accumulated plastic
strain in practice?

222
00:17:28,022 --> 00:17:31,592
First, run enough cycles. ASME requires at
least three,

223
00:17:31,642 --> 00:17:35,870
and Eurocode 3 Part 1-6 uses the increment
after the third cycle.

224
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:40,609
Second, separate the response: extract the
plastic strain increment over

225
00:17:40,659 --> 00:17:46,861
the last cycle, the plastic strain range,
and the change in key dimensions from cycle
to cycle.

226
00:17:47,361 --> 00:17:51,392
Third, apply the acceptance criteria. ASME
Section eight,

227
00:17:51,442 --> 00:17:57,190
Division two, accepts the elastic-plastic
ratcheting check if there is no plastic

228
00:17:57,240 --> 00:18:01,772
action, or an elastic core in the primary
load-bearing boundary,

229
00:18:01,822 --> 00:18:07,284
or no permanent change in the overall
dimensions. EN 13445 requires shakedown

230
00:18:07,334 --> 00:18:12,796
to be shown, for example with Melan's
theorem. Eurocode 3 Part 1-6 limits the

231
00:18:12,846 --> 00:18:18,452
total accumulated equivalent plastic strain
over the design life to twenty-five

232
00:18:18,502 --> 00:18:22,890
times the yield strain, about four point two
percent for S355.

233
00:18:22,940 --> 00:18:28,403
And ASME's local strain criterion compares
the accumulated equivalent plastic

234
00:18:28,453 --> 00:18:35,490
strain with a limit strain that falls with
stress triaxiality, adding up damage for
load sequences.

235
00:18:35,990 --> 00:18:40,612
Fourth, if the response is alternating
plasticity, compute fatigue damage from

236
00:18:40,662 --> 00:18:45,523
the stabilised strain range with a
strain–life method and add it to the high-
cycle

237
00:18:45,573 --> 00:18:50,075
damage. If it ratchets, the net strain
consumes ductility on top of fatigue.

238
00:18:50,125 --> 00:18:53,849
A simple engineering estimate adds a
ductility exhaustion term,

239
00:18:53,899 --> 00:18:57,862
the ratchet strain over the fracture strain,
to the fatigue damage.

240
00:18:57,912 --> 00:19:02,474
But in design codes, continued ratcheting is
generally simply not acceptable.

241
00:19:02,974 --> 00:19:06,809
So, is accumulated plastic strain a problem?
If it stops growing,

242
00:19:06,859 --> 00:19:10,992
the structure has shaken down, and you are
back in high-cycle fatigue,

243
00:19:11,042 --> 00:19:15,295
with the right local mean stress. If it
grows because of a closed cycle,

244
00:19:15,345 --> 00:19:19,000
it is low-cycle fatigue, governed by the
plastic strain range,

245
00:19:19,050 --> 00:19:23,243
not by the accumulated total. And if it
grows because the strain itself

246
00:19:23,293 --> 00:19:27,845
keeps drifting, that is ratcheting, and that
is the case you must design out.

247
00:19:29,395 --> 00:19:30,743
Let's put it all together.

248
00:19:31,243 --> 00:19:35,415
Start with a linear-elastic analysis. If the
equivalent stress range

249
00:19:35,465 --> 00:19:38,396
stays below twice the yield strength
everywhere,

250
00:19:38,446 --> 00:19:41,500
the structure shakes down. Use high-cycle
methods:

251
00:19:41,550 --> 00:19:47,834
local, hot-spot or notch stresses, corrected
for mean stress, with rainflow counting and
Miner's rule.

252
00:19:48,334 --> 00:19:53,408
If not, run an elastic-plastic analysis with
a cyclic material model for several

253
00:19:53,458 --> 00:19:58,147
cycles. Check ratcheting: no growth of
strains or dimensions from cycle to

254
00:19:58,197 --> 00:20:01,797
cycle, or an elastic core. Then take the
stabilised loop,

255
00:20:01,847 --> 00:20:05,448
compute the low-cycle fatigue life from its
strain range,

256
00:20:05,498 --> 00:20:11,468
check accumulated strain against local
failure limits, and add the low- and high-
cycle damage.

257
00:20:11,967 --> 00:20:15,558
High-cycle fatigue is about stress amplitude
and strength.

258
00:20:15,608 --> 00:20:19,451
Low-cycle fatigue is about plastic strain
range and ductility.

259
00:20:19,501 --> 00:20:26,985
And accumulated plastic strain only means
something once you know which of the two, or
whether ratcheting, is behind it.

260
00:20:27,485 --> 00:20:28,566
Thanks for watching.

